--- /dev/null
+++ b/ROOT-SECURITY-RELEASE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+## CVE-2025-6020 — Fix Embedded in Debian Source (pam / bookworm)
+
+| Field                | Value                                                                                     |
+|----------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
+| **CVE ID**           | CVE-2025-6020                                                                             |
+| **Component**        | pam (`pam_namespace`)                                                                     |
+| **Severity**         | HIGH (CVSS 7.8)                                                                           |
+| **Distribution**     | Debian 12 (Bookworm)                                                                      |
+| **Bookworm package** | `pam` `1.5.2-6+deb12u2`                                                                  |
+| **Upstream fixed**   | Linux-PAM 1.7.1                                                                           |
+| **Assessment**       | Fix is embedded in the deb12u2 source package. Binary rebuild required to deliver it.     |
+
+---
+
+### Summary
+
+CVE-2025-6020 is a HIGH severity local privilege escalation in `pam_namespace`.
+The vulnerable `protect_dir()` function uses pathname-based operations susceptible
+to symlink attacks and race conditions, allowing a local user to gain root.
+
+### Why a code patch is not applied here
+
+Debian `1.5.2-6+deb12u2` ships the fix in its **source package** via two embedded
+quilt patches that are applied by `dpkg-source` before the os-patcher build begins:
+
+- `upstream/0001-pam_inline-introduce-pam_asprintf-pam_snprintf-and-p.patch`
+- `upstream/0002-pam_namespace-from-v1.7.1.patch`
+
+Patch `0002` replaces `protect_dir()` with `secure_opendir()` — an fd-based path
+traversal that eliminates the symlink-attack and race-condition window (same fix as
+Linux-PAM 1.7.1 commits 475bd60c, 592d84e1, 976c2007).
+
+Re-applying a code patch on top of the already-patched source causes all hunks to
+fail (31/32 for `pam_namespace.c`). A dummy patch is used instead so that the
+os-patcher rebuild is triggered, delivering the fix that Debian's embedded patches
+provide.
+
+### Why the binary in the Debian archive is still marked vulnerable
+
+The `.deb` binary currently in the archive was built **before** Debian added these
+source patches. The Debian security tracker notes:
+*"Patch not applied correctly during build"* — the archived binary does not contain
+the fix. Rebuilding from the patched source (as os-patcher does) produces a fixed
+binary.
+
+### References
+
+- https://github.com/linux-pam/linux-pam/commit/475bd60c552b98c7eddb3270b0b4196847c0072e
+- https://github.com/linux-pam/linux-pam/commit/592d84e1265d04c3104acee815a503856db503a1
+- https://github.com/linux-pam/linux-pam/commit/976c20079358d133514568fc7fd95c02df8b5773
+- https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2025-6020
